# Line endings for files that a Linux machine executes or parses.
#
# The repo had no .gitattributes and the Windows dev box runs core.autocrlf=true,
# so install.sh, the systemd units and the Dockerfile sat in the working copy
# with CRLF. Git stored LF, which hides the problem from `git diff` — but the
# documented deploy path is "clone the repo, copy these to the server", and the
# upgrade script ships a tarball built on Windows on purpose. A shell script with
# CRLF fails on Linux as `/usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory`,
# which reads like a missing interpreter rather than a line ending.
#
# eol=lf is checkout behaviour: these files are LF on every machine regardless of
# the platform default.

*.sh        text eol=lf
*.bash      text eol=lf
*.service   text eol=lf
*.timer     text eol=lf
*.socket    text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
*.dockerfile text eol=lf

# Workflow `run:` blocks are shell scripts a Linux runner executes. YAML parsing
# normalises line breaks so CRLF here has never actually broken a build — but
# the rule in this file is "what Linux runs is LF", and an exception nobody can
# explain later is worse than a line of config.
.github/workflows/*.yml text eol=lf
.github/workflows/*.yaml text eol=lf

# Windows-side scripts stay CRLF — PowerShell is fine either way, but a .ps1 with
# LF confuses some older hosts and there is no reason to change them.
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf

# Binary: never touched, never diffed as text.
*.png       binary
*.ico       binary
*.icns      binary
*.exe       binary
*.dll       binary
*.node      binary
*.db        binary
*.traineddata binary

# Source and docs: LF in the repository, whatever the checkout does locally.
#
# Three files had reached the repo with CRLF and, in two cases, with doubled
# carriage returns - CR CR LF - a file written back through a converting
# layer more than once. Every other tracked source file is LF.
# more than once. Nothing failed because of it, but every edit to those files
# produced a diff of the whole file, which hides the actual change and is how a
# one-line fix arrives for review as four hundred.
#
# core.autocrlf is a per-machine setting and cannot be relied on; this is the
# repository saying it for everyone.
*.ts        text eol=lf
*.tsx       text eol=lf
*.mjs       text eol=lf
*.cjs       text eol=lf
*.js        text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
